1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,260
Hello, young thinkers!

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,785
Atul Sir, your Thinking Mentor, has guided this lesson.

3
00:00:08,185 --> 00:00:12,034
Today, we learn to tell three kinds of mapping apart.

4
00:00:13,384 --> 00:00:15,636
First, how to read a mapping.

5
00:00:15,886 --> 00:00:18,555
x is mapped to two x minus three.

6
00:00:18,805 --> 00:00:19,885
Put in three:

7
00:00:19,935 --> 00:00:21,993
two times three, minus three,

8
00:00:22,043 --> 00:00:23,026
gives three.

9
00:00:23,576 --> 00:00:26,298
The part after the rule is the domain.

10
00:00:26,448 --> 00:00:29,272
It tells you which inputs are allowed.

11
00:00:30,622 --> 00:00:33,467
Concept one: three kinds of mapping.

12
00:00:33,817 --> 00:00:34,538
One-one:

13
00:00:34,588 --> 00:00:37,627
each output comes from just one input.

14
00:00:38,177 --> 00:00:39,001
Many-one:

15
00:00:39,051 --> 00:00:41,177
two inputs share an output.

16
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:42,631
One-many:

17
00:00:42,681 --> 00:00:44,929
one input gives two outputs.

18
00:00:46,279 --> 00:00:49,192
Concept two: test a pair of inputs.

19
00:00:49,492 --> 00:00:52,876
Take x is mapped to x squared minus four.

20
00:00:53,176 --> 00:00:55,255
Try two, and minus two.

21
00:00:55,505 --> 00:00:59,267
Two squared is four. Minus four gives zero.

22
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:03,432
Minus two squared is also four. Zero again.

23
00:01:03,882 --> 00:01:06,571
Two different inputs, one output.

24
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:09,047
So this mapping is many-one.

25
00:01:09,897 --> 00:01:13,333
Tip: always try a pair with opposite signs.

26
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:17,233
Concept three: the line tests.

27
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:20,384
Here is x cubed minus three x.

28
00:01:20,734 --> 00:01:23,252
Draw a horizontal line across it.

29
00:01:23,452 --> 00:01:25,679
It meets the curve three times.

30
00:01:25,929 --> 00:01:28,439
Three inputs share one output:

31
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:29,313
many-one.

32
00:01:30,163 --> 00:01:32,240
Now try two x minus three.

33
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,817
Every horizontal line meets it just once.

34
00:01:36,067 --> 00:01:37,541
So it is one-one.

35
00:01:38,191 --> 00:01:42,399
A vertical line shows how many outputs one input has.

36
00:01:43,949 --> 00:01:47,437
Concept four: the domain can change the answer.

37
00:01:47,787 --> 00:01:52,371
On all real numbers, x squared minus four is many-one.

38
00:01:52,721 --> 00:01:56,798
Now allow only x greater than or equal to zero.

39
00:01:57,048 --> 00:01:59,468
The left half of the curve is gone.

40
00:01:59,718 --> 00:02:02,821
Every horizontal line now meets it once.

41
00:02:03,071 --> 00:02:06,073
Same rule, but now it is one-one.

42
00:02:06,823 --> 00:02:09,473
So always read the domain first.

43
00:02:10,923 --> 00:02:14,052
Concept five: one input, two outputs.

44
00:02:14,352 --> 00:02:17,058
Map each number to its square roots.

45
00:02:17,308 --> 00:02:18,564
Four goes to two,

46
00:02:18,564 --> 00:02:19,816
and to minus two.

47
00:02:20,116 --> 00:02:22,909
Nine goes to three, and to minus three.

48
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:25,312
One input, two outputs:

49
00:02:25,362 --> 00:02:26,766
that is one-many.

50
00:02:27,516 --> 00:02:30,557
Watch for a plus-or-minus sign in the rule.

51
00:02:32,007 --> 00:02:34,887
Now, the traps in this exercise.

52
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:39,719
In questions two and six, try inputs with opposite signs.

53
00:02:40,269 --> 00:02:43,763
In question three, odd powers keep their sign.

54
00:02:43,863 --> 00:02:45,573
Test before you decide.

55
00:02:46,123 --> 00:02:50,197
In questions five and seven, read the domain first.

56
00:02:50,747 --> 00:02:54,288
In question eight, plus-or-minus means two signs.

57
00:02:55,738 --> 00:02:56,715
Your turn.

58
00:02:56,965 --> 00:03:00,221
Here is which idea helps with which question.

59
00:03:00,971 --> 00:03:04,879
Stuck? Dyna's hints are in your ThinkBook.

60
00:03:05,229 --> 00:03:07,844
Pause the video, and try all eight!
